\details{
This is the node-level counterpart to \code{\link[=summarize_graph_metrics]{summarize_graph_metrics()}}, which
describes the graph as a whole. Having both means a ranking of nodes no longer
has to be stitched together from individual \pkg{igraph} calls, and because the
returned graph carries the metrics as vertex attributes, the result feeds
directly into \code{\link[=plot_Net]{plot_Net()}} (\code{color = "pagerank"}) and into
\code{\link[=robustness_analysis]{robustness_analysis()}} (\code{removal_strategy = "pagerank"}), which already
accepts any numeric vertex attribute as a removal priority.

The metrics are:

\describe{
\item{\code{degree}}{Number of incident edges. Always the unweighted count.}
\item{\code{strength}}{Sum of incident edge weights; equal to \code{degree} when
unweighted.}
\item{\code{betweenness}}{Fraction of shortest paths passing through the node.
Uses edge \emph{costs} when weighted.}
\item{\code{closeness}}{Reciprocal of the mean distance to all \emph{reachable}
nodes. Not comparable across components -- see below.}
\item{\code{harmonic}}{Sum of the reciprocal distances. Unlike \code{closeness} this
is comparable across components, because an unreachable pair contributes
\eqn{1/\infty = 0} rather than being excluded from the average.}
\item{\code{eigenvector}}{Leading eigenvector of the adjacency matrix: a node is
central if its neighbours are.}
\item{\code{pagerank}}{Stationary distribution of a random surfer. Sums to 1.}
\item{\code{coreness}}{Largest \emph{k} for which the node belongs to the k-core.}
\item{\code{clustering}}{Local transitivity: how interconnected the node's
neighbourhood is. \code{NaN} for nodes of degree below 2, which have no
neighbour pairs.}
\item{\code{constraint}}{Burt's constraint: how much the node's connections are
concentrated within a single group. Low constraint marks a broker.}
\item{\code{eccentricity}}{Distance to the furthest reachable node.}
}

\code{"closeness"} is omitted from the defaults because it is not comparable across
components, and most real networks are disconnected. \pkg{igraph} averages the
distance over reachable vertices only, so a node in a \emph{small} isolated
component scores \emph{higher} than an equally well-placed node in the giant
component -- everything in its component is nearby. Ranking by closeness on a
fragmented graph therefore puts the periphery on top. \code{"harmonic"} answers the
same question without that failure mode, because unreachable pairs contribute
zero rather than being dropped from the denominator. Request \code{"closeness"}
explicitly if the graph is connected or you want the per-component reading.

The default plot is a correlation heatmap rather than a ranking because the
most common mistake with a table like this is to treat the metrics as
independent evidence. On many networks betweenness, eigenvector centrality and
PageRank correlate with degree above 0.9, so a node that looks important by
four measures may be important by one. The heatmap makes that visible before
the ranking is interpreted.
}
\section{Edge weights}{


Functions that can use edge weights take two arguments:

\describe{
\item{\code{weights}}{\code{NULL} (the default) to ignore edge weights; the name of an
edge attribute, such as \code{"weight"}; or a numeric vector with one value per
edge, in \code{igraph::E(graph)} order.}
\item{\code{weight_type}}{\code{"strength"} (the default) if a larger value means a
more tightly connected pair -- confidence scores, correlations,
co-expression, read counts, interaction scores. \code{"distance"} if a larger
value means further apart -- costs, dissimilarities, reaction times.}
}

Declaring which you have is not bookkeeping. \pkg{igraph} reads the \code{weight}
attribute implicitly and gives it \emph{opposite} meanings in different functions:
a cost in \code{\link[igraph:betweenness]{igraph::betweenness()}}, \code{\link[igraph:distances]{igraph::distances()}},
\code{\link[igraph:diameter]{igraph::diameter()}} and \code{\link[igraph:distances]{igraph::mean_distance()}}, but a strength in
\code{\link[igraph:cluster_louvain]{igraph::cluster_louvain()}} and the other community detection algorithms.
Attaching a confidence score and letting that happen implicitly therefore
inverts every path-based metric -- a high-confidence interaction is treated as
a long distance -- while community detection reads the same numbers the way
you intended.

netkit resolves \code{weights} and \code{weight_type} once per call and derives both a
strength and a distance vector from them, so each metric receives the one it
needs. A \code{"strength"} is converted to a distance by reciprocal
(\eqn{1/w}); a \code{"distance"} is converted to a strength by reflection
(\eqn{\max(w) - w + \min(w)}), which keeps a zero distance finite.
}

